pjmedia: denoise the capture path with RNNoise, once per device rather than once per leg

Background noise goes out on every call. A fan, a street, a railway platform, a radio in
the next room: all of it is captured, all of it is encoded, and at Lyra's 3.2 kbit/s it is
worse than merely transmitted. Lyra is generative - it resynthesises speech from a learned
prior rather than coding the waveform - so noise at its input does not pass through, it
spends bits and steers the generator. Cleaning up afterwards is not an option either,
because by then the noise is part of what the decoder invented.

So the denoiser goes in the capture path, and this patch puts it in the one place in
pjmedia where every captured frame passes exactly once.

## Where, and why there

`rec_cb()` in pjmedia/src/pjmedia/sound_port.c, between `pjmedia_echo_capture()` and
`pjmedia_port_put_frame()`.

  - After the echo canceller, because a denoiser must not be asked to model the far end's
    voice as noise.
  - Before `pjmedia_port_put_frame()`, because that call is the conference bridge.
    Everything downstream of it is per-leg: in this app's four-party mesh there are three
    encoders and three RTP streams, and a denoiser placed after the bridge would run three
    times on the same microphone audio. Here the cost is constant in party count.

This is deliberately NOT a pjmedia_port. A port would have to be spliced into the capture
chain, which adds a frame of buffering and a place for the chain to be assembled wrongly;
`pjmedia_snd_port` already owns the echo canceller at exactly this point and owning a
second processor beside it changes no topology at all.

## What was measured, and what it replaced

Nothing here was chosen from a paper. tools/speech-enhancement compiles the *vendored*
denoiser sources for the host, drives them through the same call sequence the device uses,
and scores the output with DNSMOS P.835. Clean speech mixed with seven real noise classes
at 0, 5 and 10 dB SNR; the numbers below are the mean over all seven at 5 dB, as SIG/BAK:

    clean reference (the ceiling)      3.62 / 3.99
    unprocessed                        2.57 / 2.06
    WebRTC NS, as pjmedia runs it      2.57 / 2.33        4.6 dB attenuation
    WebRTC NS at policy 3              2.77 / 3.09       10.5 dB
    WebRTC NS at policy 3, 16 kHz      2.99 / 2.95       11.9 dB
    RNNoise                            3.38 / 3.93       26.8 dB

Three findings, and each one closed an option that looked reasonable beforehand.

**pjmedia's existing noise suppressor cannot do this job, and it is not a tuning problem.**
`echo_webrtc.c` creates WebRTC's noise suppressor under PJMEDIA_ECHO_USE_NOISE_SUPPRESSOR
and then never calls `WebRtcNs_set_policy`, so it runs at the `aggrMode = 0` that
`WebRtcNs_InitCore` assigns (ns_core.c:134). Mode 0 sets `denoiseBound = 0.5f`, a floor of
0.5 under the Wiener gain, which caps attenuation near 6 dB however loud the noise is -
4.6 dB measured. It is a real improvement over nothing and it is an order of magnitude
short of what is needed, and on music and bells it does essentially nothing at all
(BAK 1.30 and 1.19 against an unprocessed 1.15 and 1.17).

**Raising that policy is not the answer either.** Mode 3 buys 10.5 dB and spends 0.6 of
SIG on the four ordinary noise classes doing it - on fan noise, 3.47/3.23 becomes
2.69/3.44. A suppressor that trades speech for silence at that rate is not a better
suppressor.

**Part of why is that pjmedia drives it at the wrong block size, and that is worth
recording even though this patch does not fix it.** `echo_webrtc.c:158` sets
`subframe_len = 160` for every clock rate above 8000 and passes `num_bands = 1`. At 16 kHz
that is the 10 ms frame `ns_core.c` says it supports; at 48 kHz - this app's
CORE_CLOCK_RATE - the same 160 samples are 3.33 ms, so every time constant in the noise
estimator advances three times per 10 ms of audio. WebRTC's own audio processing module
band-splits above 16 kHz and never does this. Running the identical policy at 16 kHz
instead recovers 0.22 of SIG and 1.4 dB, which confirms the geometry is a second defect in
its own right. It is left alone here because the backend that replaces it does not have the
problem: RNNoise is natively 48 kHz in 480-sample blocks, and 960 samples per 20 ms frame
is exactly two of them.

**RNNoise reaches the clean reference on both axes.** BAK 3.93 against the ceiling's 3.99,
and on fan, traffic, train and street it scores *above* the clean reference (4.08, 4.15,
4.06, 4.07) because it also strips the corpus's own recording noise floor. SIG costs 0.24,
which is at the edge of DNSMOS's own run-to-run spread. On music it is 3.31/3.61 where
unprocessed is 1.33/1.15, and on bells 3.34/3.82 where unprocessed is 1.27/1.17 - the two
cases a statistical noise-floor estimator has no mechanism for.

Note what the SIG column says about absolute thresholds. A target of "SIG >= 3.8" is not
reachable on this corpus by any denoiser, because the clean, unmixed speech scores 3.62:
MS-SNSD is downsampled VCTK and carries its own noise floor. The criterion that survives
contact with a ceiling is SIG within DNSMOS's noise of the clean reference and BAK at least
the clean reference's, and that is what is met.

## Why RNNoise and not DeepFilterNet3

DeepFilterNet3 is the better denoiser on paper and was the first choice. It is a PyTorch
model with no official TFLite export, and its deep-filtering stage is built on
complex-valued operations that the torch -> onnx -> tf -> tflite route does not carry
across intact. Putting it on this handset is a model-conversion project with an uncertain
outcome, not an integration task.

RNNoise is the opposite trade. The weights are already in the source tree
(src/rnnoise_data.c, 4.9 MB of them), so there is no model file to convert, package into
the APK, or fail to load at runtime - and the TFLite runtime Lyra already links is not
dragged into the audio capture path. It is natively 48 kHz in 10 ms blocks, so nothing
resamples and nothing reframes. `vec_neon.h` means the arm64 build is vectorised. And it is
BSD-licensed, which makes it the one dependency in this tree that adds no obligation to
ADR-002's unresolved GPLv2 position.

It is also a 2017-generation GRU with a small model, and the table above is what it is
worth rather than what it is reputed to be.

## The switch, and why it is a bit in ec_options

PJMEDIA_ECHO_USE_SPEECH_ENHANCER, bit 11 of the same word that already carries the echo
canceller's options.

That word is the one pjsua already carries from the application down to
`pjmedia_snd_port`: `pjsua_media_config.ec_options` becomes
`pjmedia_snd_port_param.ec_options` (pjsua_aud.c:1032) becomes
`snd_port->prm_ec_options`. A new field in `pjsua_media_config` would be the tidier home
and it would also be an ABI change plus a regenerated SWIG surface - which drags the
swig-4.2.0 pin, and every JNI symbol name that depends on it, into what is otherwise a
boolean. Bit 11 rather than the lowest free bit (512) because an upstream addition to
`pjmedia_echo_flag` would take the lowest, and `tools/vendor/apply-patches.sh` runs
`patch -F0`, so a collision there fails the patch instead of silently relocating it.

The flag also suppresses PJMEDIA_ECHO_USE_NOISE_SUPPRESSOR whenever the enhancer is
created successfully, so that two noise suppressors can never run in series - cascading
them over-attenuates, and costs more than half a MOS point of SIG for a fraction of a point
of BAK.

On THIS build that guard is unexercised, and the reason is worth recording next to it.
PJMEDIA_ECHO_USE_NOISE_SUPPRESSOR is read in exactly one place, echo_webrtc.c:192, so it is
a WebRTC-backend flag - and the WebRTC backend aborts the process at 48 kHz.
`WebRtcAec_Init(inst, clock_rate, clock_rate)` makes WebRTC compute
`aec->num_bands = sampFreq / 16000` = 3 (aec_core.c:1547), and `webrtc_aec_cancel_echo`
then passes `echo->channel_count` - the CHANNEL count, 1 - into the parameter WebRTC reads
as the BAND count (echo_webrtc.c:326). `assert(aec->num_bands == num_bands)` at
aec_core.c:1765 is not compiled out, and the app dies in the capture callback on the first
frame of the first call. Verified on a Samsung M23, 2026-10-04.

So the application sets the enhancer bit alone, leaves the algorithm field at
PJMEDIA_ECHO_DEFAULT - which echo_common.c's `else if` chain resolves to Speex, tested at
:212 before WebRTC at :219 - and the fallback behind the enhancer is no denoising rather
than a weaker denoiser. The guard stays because it is correct and costs three lines, and
because a pjproject bump that fixes the band count would make it live again.

## The fallback is the absence of a branch

PJMEDIA_HAS_RNNOISE 0 compiles speech_enh.c's second half: `pjmedia_speech_enh_create()`
returns PJ_ENOTSUP, `snd_port->speech_enh` stays NULL, and `rec_cb()` is byte-for-byte the
callback that shipped before this patch. The same path is taken when the backend IS
compiled in but refuses the stream - a clock rate that is not 48 kHz, more than one
channel, or a frame that is not a whole number of 480-sample blocks. Each refusal logs
which format it refused, at level 3, because a silently absent denoiser is indistinguishable
from a denoiser that is not working.

A create failure is never propagated. `start_sound_device()` discards the status and goes
on, so a denoiser that cannot start costs the call nothing beyond the denoising.

## Cost, and the thread it runs on

`pjmedia_speech_enh_capture()` runs on the capture callback thread and carries no lock,
exactly as `pjmedia_echo_capture()` does two lines above it. The recurrent state has one
writer and one reader and they are the same thread. The int16-to-float scratch is allocated
once at create time from the port's pool: there is no allocation per frame.

Upstream: not sent. RNNoise is not a dependency pjproject has, and the ec_options bit is a
local accommodation of pjsua's existing plumbing rather than a design upstream would want.
